            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You are fixing a narrative inconsistency in a research paper about \"Depth Profiling\" (DDR - Differential Depth Response).\n\nFile: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_3/projects/gen-depth-fingerprint/docs/paper/results.tex\n\n## Problem\nThe text mixes \"pooled d\" and \"per-generator d\" without clarification, creating a logical contradiction for reviewers:\n\n1. Around line 61/72: Text says \"three near-stationary features (d < 0.10)\" — this is the pooled Cohen's d, which is correct.\n2. Around line 84-85: Text says tail decay \"passes TOST equivalence for only 4/8 generators (|d|=0.10--0.22)\" — this is per-generator d range.\n\nA reviewer seeing \"d < 0.10\" and then \"d=0.10-0.22\" in the same section will perceive a contradiction.\n\nAdditionally, the stated range \"|d|=0.10--0.22\" may not match the TOST appendix data. The actual per-generator full range is 0.07-0.22, and the non-passing generators have d=0.15-0.22.\n\n## Fix needed\nRead the file. In the tail decay sentence (around line 85), add a brief qualifier like \"per-generator\" to distinguish from pooled d, and verify/correct the stated range. The fix should be minimal — just adding \"per-generator\" before the d range, something like:\n\n\"tail decay passes two one-sided tests (TOST) equivalence (Appendix~\\ref{app:tost}) for only 4/8 generators (per-generator $|d|{=}0.10$--$0.22$)\"\n\nRead the file first, then make the minimal edit."
            }
          ],
